## **3\. Background Canvas Rules**

The deepest structural layer of the application is the Background Canvas. This layer is entirely static, deeply matte, and establishes the physical boundaries of the digital workspace. It serves as the architectural void over which the interface elements float.

### **3.1 The Matte Slate-Navy Base and Micro-Grid**

The root element of the application relies on a composite background image property to achieve its precise texture. The foundation is a radial gradient (radial-gradient(circle at 50% 0%, \#121724 0%, \#05060A 100%)) that provides a soft, localized illumination at the top center of the viewport, fading into absolute slate-carbon at the periphery.  
Overlaid on this gradient is a custom 2px by 2px architectural micro-grid. This is achieved using two intersecting linear gradients: linear-gradient(to right, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.012) 1px, transparent 1px) and linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.012) 1px, transparent 1px). Sized precisely at 24px 24px with a screen blend mode, this grid provides an almost imperceptible architectural texture that grounds the application without distracting the user.

### **3.2 The Q3 Locked Watermark**

A defining visual signature of the V3 system is the locked watermark. The ci-angel.webp asset must be permanently anchored in Quadrant 3 (the bottom-left corner of the viewport). The implementation requires exact positioning: bottom: '-8vh', left: '-8vw', with a physical dimension of 55vmin by 55vmin.  
Crucially, the opacity of this watermark is strictly codified at 0.33 (33%). It must exist at zIndex: 1, ensuring it sits permanently behind all glass surfaces. When a translucent glass card passes over or sits above the watermark, the underlying image is naturally and beautifully blurred, reinforcing the physical depth of the interface.

### **3.3 Immutability**

The background canvas does not transition, scroll, or animate under any circumstances. It remains entirely fixed (position: relative on the container, but acting as a fixed viewport). The illusion of page navigation and all multipage transitions occur entirely within the glass layers hovering above this canvas, preserving the foundational stability of the environment.

## **4\. The 77.7% Main Glass Card Contract**

The most prominent and defining visual component of the V3 Veil Glass system is the Main Glass Card. Every primary operational surface—dashboards, calendars, compliance intelligence panels, and policy workspaces—is housed within this centralized, structured contract.3

### **4.1 Structural Dimensions and Positioning**

The main card acts as the primary content vessel. On desktop viewports, its width is strictly defined as 77.7% of the available space, with a minimum width of 980px to protect complex data tables and Kanban interfaces from collapsing. The height is locked to 92vh, centered within the viewport. On mobile devices, the card expands to 100% width and 100vh height, snapping to the edges of the display and dropping its 24px border radius in favor of a flush 0px radius.

### **4.2 The Glassmorphism Equation**

The visual density and physical presence of the main glass card are governed by the strict "77.7% Contract." This parameter dictates that the card must read visually as a highly opaque, matte glass surface relative to the slate-navy background.  
The background property relies on a sophisticated specular gradient: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(32, 41, 56, 0.88) 0%, rgba(16, 20, 28, 0.45) 60%, rgba(8, 10, 13, 0.98) 100%). This creates a bright navy-grey top-left origin, transitioning through a semi-transparent midpoint, and resolving into a deep black-charcoal at the bottom-right.  
To achieve the frosted glass effect, the CSS property backdropFilter: 'blur(32px) saturate(140%)' (with its \-webkit- vendor prefix counterpart) is applied. This intense blur diffuses the underlying 2x2 grid and the Q3 watermark, while the 140% saturation slightly elevates the vibrancy of any colors passing behind the card.

### **4.3 Border and Shadow Properties**

To define the edges of the glass without relying on harsh, solid lines, the system previously utilized borders, but V3 specifically dictates border: 'none' on the main container itself, relying instead on the internal components to catch the light. A realistic cast shadow, boxShadow: '30px 10px 80px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.9)', is applied to the right side and bottom of the card. This deep, aggressive shadow physically separates the 77.7% card from the background canvas, selling the illusion of a heavy, hovering pane of glass.

## **5\. Navigation System**

The navigation architecture functions as the central nervous system of the multipage application. As observed in the detailed design references, the system utilizes a left-aligned vertical sidebar containing highly structured primary and secondary routing nodes categorized into distinct operational domains (Primary Operations, Compliance Execution, Administration & Knowledge).3

### **5.1 Invisible Surface Integration**

The sidebar explicitly rejects the use of a bordered glass card. Instead, it utilizes an entirely transparent container (background: 'transparent', borderRight: 'none'). This integrates the navigation directly with the matte slate-navy background, establishing a clear spatial hierarchy: the navigation feels foundational and native to the canvas, while the main content area (the 77.7% glass card) feels elevated and distinct.

### **5.2 Dynamic Width and Animation**

The sidebar state is controlled via the isNavOpen boolean. When active, the navigation expands to 260px; when closed, it collapses to 0px. This dimensional shift is accompanied by a beautifully engineered transition: transition: 'width 0.7s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1), min-width 0.7s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1), opacity 0.7s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)'. This specific bezier curve ensures a rapid initial slide that elegantly decelerates into its final position, pushing the main glass card dynamically to the right.

### **5.3 Active States and Submenus**

Active navigation states are subtle. An active item receives a rgba(0, 209, 193, 0.1) background fill, while its icon transitions to the tealLight token. The text weight increases from 500 to 600\.  
Submenus (e.g., Clinical Profiles, Taxonomy Map) are handled via collapsible accordion structures. When expanded, submenu items are indented and accompanied by a beautiful nested left border (borderLeft: '1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06)'), creating a clear visual hierarchy of nested routes without cluttering the interface.

### **5.4 The Vertical Light-Catching Divider**

A defining structural accent is the interrupted vertical divider line separating the navigation from the main card. Rendered conditionally when isNavOpen is true, this 1px wide element (background: 'rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.12)') acts as a catch-light. It does not connect perfectly to the top and bottom edges of the viewport, maintaining a floating, elegant presence that smoothly transitions into visibility alongside the sidebar.

## **6\. Strict Color & Accent Strategy**

The V3 system requires draconian restraint in the application of color. The interface is designed to feel mature, clinical, and high-end. Random splashes of color destroy the precision of the glassmorphic aesthetic.

### **6.1 Teal as the Singular Truth**

The primary interactive color across the entire application is the deep, luminous brand Teal (\#00D1C1). Every active line, overdue status label, progress completion state, and primary icon has been mapped to this color. The previous implementation's reliance on disparate semantic colors (red for errors, standard orange for warnings) has been completely eliminated in favor of a monochromatic accent strategy. This forces the user to rely on iconography and typography for context, resulting in a cleaner, less alarming interface. For example, overdue tasks in the My Planner view are no longer red or orange; they are outlined in rgba(0, 209, 193, 0.33) with a 0.02 opacity teal background.

### **6.2 The Orange Command Center Exception**

There is exactly one exception to the Teal dominance rule: The "Command Center" and "My Personal Workspace" tags. To establish absolute focal hierarchy, these specific elements utilize the orangeLight (\#FFA059) token. This is not merely a color application; it requires a specialized glowing radiant neon light-tube text shadow effect: textShadow: '0 0 10px rgba(255, 160, 89, 0.95), 0 0 20px rgba(255, 160, 89, 0.45)'. This creates a luminous, backlit effect that draws the eye immediately to the most critical operational data without relying on heavy, opaque background boxes.

