## **15\. Anti-Patterns**

To preserve the elite, premium feel of the V3 system and prevent the interface from degrading into a disorganized, amateur execution, developers and designers must strictly avoid the following critical anti-patterns:

| Anti-Pattern | Description | Correction Strategy |
| :---- | :---- | :---- |
| **The "Bounce" Effect** | Utilizing spring-like or elastic easings (cubic-bezier curves that overshoot 1.0) on page transitions. | The interface is composed of heavy glass panels, not rubber. Use the strict deceleration and standard curves defined in Section 11.6. |
| **Opaque Data Containers** | Applying a 100% solid background color (e.g., a solid dark grey box) to a card or list item that sits on top of the 77.7% glass card. | This instantly destroys the volumetric depth of the application. Utilize the transparent .v3-invisible-glare pattern or a maximum 0.015 opacity fill. |
| **Excessive Transition Naming** | Manually assigning a unique view-transition-name to every single item in a massive list, resulting in CSS bloat and frame drops. | Rely on the new match-element capability or restrict naming to the parent wrapper for grouped transitions. |
| **Animation Collisions** | Allowing a CSS View Transition and a heavy GSAP staggered entrance to fire at the exact same millisecond. | Chain animations logically. Await the viewTransition.finished promise before triggering the interior layout GSAP animations. |
| **Orphaned Watermarks** | Layering the Q3 watermark *above* a glass card or applying zIndex values that cause it to sit on top of content. | The watermark must never obstruct data. It belongs to the absolute lowest stratum of the background canvas. |

